---
/**
 * The card masonry — a three-column grid whose cards pack by measured row spans.
 *
 * ── EXTRACTED FROM `insights/index.astro`, FIRST CONSUMER IS PRESENTATIONS ───
 *
 * The first of the three components the index apparatus is being split into
 * (<FacetFilters>, <CardMasonry>, <ResultsFooter>). It is built here because the
 * Presentations index needs a masonry NOW and the alternative was a throwaway second
 * copy of the script — so the copy that gets written is the one that survives.
 *
 * INSIGHTS HAS NOT ADOPTED IT YET, and that is deliberate rather than unfinished. Its
 * grid is coupled to its filter in two ways this component does not yet answer: the
 * filter calls `relayout()` after changing which cards are hidden, and the truncation
 * rule (`html.js .masonry:not([data-hydrated]) > :nth-child(n + 25)`) belongs to the
 * reveal rather than to the grid. Both want designing with both pages in view, which is
 * the extraction change. `onRelayout` below is the provisional answer to the first.
 *
 * ── EVERY CHILD SELECTOR IS `:global()`, AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL ──────────────
 *
 * The cards arrive through <slot />, and SLOTTED CONTENT CARRIES THE CALLER'S SCOPE,
 * not this component's. So `.card-masonry > :nth-child(3n + 1)` compiles to
 * `…[data-astro-cid-THIS] > …[data-astro-cid-THIS]`, the card carries the PAGE's cid,
 * and the rule matches nothing — silently. It compiles, it ships, and the columns
 * simply never apply.
 *
 * `:global()` is therefore wrapped around the child half of every one of these
 * selectors, anchored under `.card-masonry`, which IS this component's root and so
 * still carries the scope. That keeps the rules from leaking to other grids while
 * letting them reach the slotted cards. This was the failure CLAUDE.md names; it was
 * verified in the built CSS rather than assumed — see the handoff.
 *
 * ── THE DOM IS IN DATE ORDER, AND EVERYTHING FOLLOWS FROM THAT ───────────────
 *
 * Native CSS masonry does not ship, so three columns are built by hand. Cards are
 * emitted FLAT, in date order, into one grid; columns come from `nth-child`, and the
 * packing from `grid-auto-rows: 1px` plus a per-card `grid-row: span`. The rejected
 * alternative was a build-time partition into three column wrappers, which forces a
 * contiguous DOM slice per column and therefore a CSS `order` per card — buying visual
 * order at the cost of a focus mismatch, non-chronological screen-reader traversal, and
 * a future `h-feed` out of date order.
 *
 * MEASURED ON THE REAL 45-CARD INSIGHTS CORPUS, all three rungs, before that switch:
 *
 *                        ragged   wasted gap   height    DOM order
 *   3 col, spans           219        0        6686      date
 *   3 col, old wrappers    241        0        6686      partition
 *   2 col, spans            89        0        9872      date
 *   2 col, old wrappers    447      876       10489      partition
 *   1 col, either            0        0       19687      date / partition
 *
 * The two-column rung is where it stops being close: the old approach could not serve a
 * second partition from one DOM, so it fell back to a row grid there. This does not have
 * that problem, which also retired the "tablet row gaps" question.
 *
 * ── COLUMNS MOVED OUT OF `nth-child` ON 2026-09-17, AND THE ABOVE STILL HOLDS ─
 *
 * Everything above is about DOM order and spans, and none of it changed. What changed is
 * WHICH COLUMN a card lands in, and only in the measured state: `nth-child` still owns
 * the no-JS and pre-measure states, the script owns the state after it has measured.
 *
 * `nth-child` is a strict ROUND-ROBIN, and a round-robin has no mechanism to correct a
 * coincidence. Presentations found the coincidence. Its cards are BIMODAL rather than
 * varied — a poster makes a card ~362px and its absence ~128px, a step change, not a
 * gradient — and five of the six posterless presentations happen to sit at `i % 3 == 0`:
 *
 *                  boxed   bare
 *     column 1       3      5
 *     column 2       7      1
 *     column 3       8      0
 *
 * At ~234px apiece that is the whole 1092px ragged bottom, measured on screen. Note that
 * `grid-auto-flow: dense` cannot touch it: every card has a DEFINITE `grid-column`, so
 * dense only ever backfills within a column.
 *
 * ── THIS IS NOT A REVERSAL OF `4bed985`, WHICH IS THE EASY MISREADING ────────
 *
 * A greedy shortest-column partition existed and was deleted. It was deleted because it
 * ran at BUILD TIME, where heights must be estimated and an under-estimate makes a card
 * overflow its neighbour — the paragraph below. It was not deleted because greedy
 * balancing was found wanting; `4bed985`'s numbers say spans beat wrappers, not that
 * balancing was wrong. The script already measures every card exactly, so applying
 * greedy to MEASURED heights is the one place that objection does not reach.
 *
 * A second, client-side partition was retired in `d091932`, again for an unrelated
 * reason: it read its column count back from `gridTemplateColumns`, which reports
 * implicit tracks and so latched onto a track that should not have existed. The trap is
 * real and this avoids it — see the script, which derives the count from geometry.
 *
 * MEASURED BEFORE AND AFTER, on the real corpora at every rung (ragged bottom / grid
 * height, px). Median gap stays 32–33 and overlap stays zero throughout:
 *
 *                              before        after
 *   Presentations 24, 3 col   1092 / 3311   291 / 2966
 *   Presentations 25, 3 col    701 / 3311   218 / 3066
 *   Presentations 25, 2 col    813 / 4907   293 / 4647
 *   Insights 24, 3 col         116 / 3275   174 / 3277
 *   Insights 42, 3 col         139 / 5914   224 / 5943
 *   Insights 42, 2 col         177 / 8840   177 / 8840
 *   either, 1 col                0            0
 *
 * ── INSIGHTS GETS SLIGHTLY WORSE, AND THAT IS THE REAL TRADE ────────────────
 *
 * Not noise, and not hidden here: +58px and +85px at three columns. Greedy BOUNDS the
 * worst case — the rag cannot exceed the tallest card — but it is myopic, and it cannot
 * beat a round-robin that a homogeneous corpus already happened to balance. Insights
 * cards vary smoothly in height, so round-robin was near-optimal there by luck.
 *
 * The trade is 800px off the corpus that is visibly broken against ~70px onto one where
 * the rag is 2–4% of the grid and was never the complaint. Taken deliberately (Andy,
 * 2026-09-17). If Insights ever becomes the problem, the thing to question is greedy
 * itself, not this file's plumbing.
 *
 * What it also costs: a card can now move further from its `nth-child` position than
 * dense's two slots — up to two columns on the current Presentations set. DOM order is
 * untouched, so screen-reader traversal and a future `h-feed` stay chronological — the
 * same trade dense already made, taken one step further.
 *
 * ── THE NO-SCRIPT STATE IS A ROW GRID, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN ────────────────
 *
 * The spans need MEASURED heights, and measuring has to happen before the spans exist:
 * once `grid-auto-rows: 1px` is in effect a card's grid area IS its span, so measuring
 * then returns the span rather than the content. Build-time estimates were tried and
 * cannot be made safe — any under-estimate makes a card overflow into its neighbour, and
 * padding the estimate to guarantee over-estimation lands back at the row grid's gaps
 * with extra steps.
 *
 * So with no JavaScript this is a row grid: correct order, nearly flush at the bottom
 * (29px on that corpus), and airier than intended — a 59px median gap against the
 * design's 32, about 16% of extra height. Accepted (Andy, 2026-08-28). Content is never
 * hidden by it.
 */
interface Props {
  /**
   * How many columns at the widest rung. Both index pages use 3; the prop exists
   * because the ladder is the one thing DESIGN.md says differs per index ("Card grids
   * — the breakpoint ladder differs per index"), and Home's card band is a plausible
   * third consumer at 2.
   */
  columns?: 2 | 3
  /**
   * Hide everything past the first reveal step before any script runs. Set it on an
   * index whose <ResultsFooter> offers "Show more" / "Show all"; see the rule below for
   * why the truncation has to be in force at the FIRST paint rather than applied by JS.
   */
  truncate?: boolean
  class?: string
  /* Forwarded to the root — grid placement from the page, and the scope attribute that
     lets a page style this component at all. */
  [key: string]: unknown
}

const {columns = 3, truncate = false, class: className, ...rest}: Props = Astro.props
---

<div
  class:list={['card-masonry', className]}
  data-card-masonry
  data-columns={columns}
  data-truncate={truncate ? '' : undefined}
  {...rest}
>
  <slot />
</div>

<style>
  .card-masonry {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;

    /* Horizontal is grid, vertical is rhythm. The boards draw 24 between columns and
       32 between cards, which is exactly this pair. `row-gap` applies in the row-grid
       state; once spans arrive the gap is built into the span instead. */
    column-gap: var(--grid-gutter);
    row-gap: var(--rhythm-list);

    /*
     * REQUIRED, at every rung. In the row-grid state it stops cards stretching to their
     * row's height — a bare card stretched to a boxed card's height would reveal that
     * whole box on hover. In the span state it stops a card stretching to fill its span,
     * which is what leaves the trailing 32 as the gap.
     */
    align-items: start;
  }

  /*
   * ── THE LADDER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Mobile-first, reading the `--breakpoint-*` flags rather than restating 48rem and
   * 64rem, so tokens.css stays the only place those widths are written.
   *
   * COLUMNS COME FROM `nth-child`, NOT FROM A DATA ATTRIBUTE PER CARD. The cycle is
   * exactly a card's position among its siblings, which `nth-child` reads directly —
   * nothing to generate, nothing in the markup, and it cannot drift out of step with
   * source order.
   *
   * ── AND `:not(.is-measured)` IS WHERE THAT STOPS BEING TRUE ─────────────────
   *
   * The ladder owns the no-JS state and the window before the script has measured. Once
   * it has, the script assigns columns from measured heights and this must get out of the
   * way. Two separate reasons, and both matter:
   *
   *   STRUCTURAL   Exactly one mechanism assigns columns at any moment, so the two can
   *                never disagree mid-handover. This file already holds that precedent —
   *                the truncation rule below carries `:not([data-hydrated])` for exactly
   *                the same reason.
   *   PRACTICAL    `:nth-child(An+B of S)` takes the pseudo-class's specificity PLUS the
   *                most specific compound in S, so `of :not([hidden])` puts these rules
   *                at (0,4,0) — above anything the measured state could write from a
   *                class selector. Without this the script would have to fight it with an
   *                inline `grid-column`, which is the silent-disagreement shape again.
   *
   * It goes on the `grid-column` rules ONLY. `grid-template-columns` is true in both
   * states and must stay unscoped, or the measured grid would collapse to one column.
   * (The reason a DEFINITE column matters at all is in the `is-measured` block, which is
   * now the only state where spans and columns coexist.)
   *
   * ── `of :not([hidden])` IS WHAT MAKES A FILTER WORK BEFORE THE SCRIPT DOES ───
   *
   * Plain `nth-child` counts every sibling, hidden ones included, so a filtered view
   * would keep whatever columns its survivors held in the full set — at worst leaving
   * all of them in one column. Measured on Insights before it was added: a 1789px
   * ragged bottom against 478 once the count is taken over the visible set alone.
   *
   * STILL LOAD-BEARING, THOUGH THE SCRIPT NOW RE-ASSIGNS ON EVERY FILTER CHANGE. This is
   * what the no-JS page gets, and it is what holds between a filter change and the
   * relayout that follows it. Without it that window shows the all-in-one-column state,
   * which is a visible collapse rather than a reflow.
   *
   * Baseline widely available — Safari 9, Chrome 111, Firefox 113.
   */
  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .card-masonry {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }

    .card-masonry:not(.is-measured) > :global(:nth-child(2n + 1 of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .card-masonry:not(.is-measured) > :global(:nth-child(2n of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 2;
    }
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-lg: true) {
    .card-masonry[data-columns='3'] {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    }

    .card-masonry[data-columns='3']:not(.is-measured)
      > :global(:nth-child(3n + 1 of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 1;
    }

    .card-masonry[data-columns='3']:not(.is-measured)
      > :global(:nth-child(3n + 2 of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 2;
    }

    .card-masonry[data-columns='3']:not(.is-measured) > :global(:nth-child(3n of :not([hidden]))) {
      grid-column: 3;
    }
  }

  /*
   * ── THE JS-ENHANCED STATE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Added by the script once it has measured every card. 1px rows are small enough that
   * no two cards can share one, so the shared-row height that makes the base state a row
   * grid stops existing. `row-gap: 0` because the 32 is inside each span.
   *
   * `dense` was chosen on the eye and is paid for in tab order. Sparse packing
   * guarantees a card never appears above an earlier one, but lets a card be held down
   * by a taller card in a PREVIOUS column — measured on a corpus with one unusually tall
   * card, sparse left a 287.75px worst gap and 511px of total excess against dense's
   * 32.88 and zero. Dense can float a later card above an earlier one (21 of 45
   * positions differed on Insights, no card moving more than two slots); DOM order is
   * untouched, so screen-reader traversal and a future `h-feed` stay chronological.
   *
   * ── `dense` WAS REMOVED ON 2026-09-17, AND ITS JOB WAS ABSORBED ─────────────
   *
   * Kept above rather than deleted, because the reasoning is what says why it can go:
   * dense existed to stop a card being held down by a taller card in a PREVIOUS column.
   * Greedy assignment makes that situation unreachable — a card goes to whichever column
   * is shortest AT THAT MOMENT, so no column is ever waiting on another. Verified rather
   * than reasoned: toggling `grid-auto-flow` on the live grid after the switch gave
   * byte-identical rag and height on both corpora at three columns. It is inert.
   *
   * So this is "its purpose was absorbed", not "it was wrong". If greedy is ever reverted,
   * dense comes back with it — the two are not independent.
   *
   * ── EVERY CARD'S COLUMN IS DEFINITE, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING ──────────────
   *
   * `--card-column` is written by the script; the ladder above is scoped away in this
   * state. A DEFINITE column is what keeps the columns independent once the spans arrive:
   * a card whose column sits left of the placement cursor pushes the cursor down a row, so
   * each column stacks on its own rather than sharing a row height.
   *
   * The fallback is not decoration. If the script has added `is-measured` but not written
   * `--card-column` — which cannot happen in one pass, but could if this rule and the
   * script ever drift — `auto` places cards left-to-right in source order, which is a
   * legible grid rather than every card stacked in column 1.
   */
  .card-masonry.is-measured {
    grid-auto-rows: 1px;
    row-gap: 0;
  }

  .card-masonry.is-measured > :global(*) {
    grid-row: span var(--card-span);
    grid-column: var(--card-column, auto);
  }

  /*
   * ── THE FIRST 24, BEFORE ANY SCRIPT RUNS ────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * The truncation has to be in force at the FIRST paint or it is a visible collapse
   * rather than an initial state — a whole index drawn and then thrown away.
   * `html.js` is set by the synchronous inline script in BaseLayout, which runs while
   * <head> is still parsing, so this rule is true before <body> exists.
   *
   * THE FOUR PARTS ARE FOUR SEPARATE JOBS:
   *
   *   html.js               only where a script can undo it. Without JS the class never
   *                         arrives, the rule never matches, and every card renders — no
   *                         control the visitor cannot operate, and nothing unreachable.
   *   [data-truncate]       opt-in. An index with no reveal controls must not hide cards
   *                         it can never show again — the Presentations index has fewer
   *                         cards than one step, so this is inert there, but a page
   *                         without <ResultsFooter> would be broken by it.
   *   :not([data-hydrated]) hands over cleanly. card-index.ts sets that attribute in the
   *                         same synchronous pass in which it starts managing `hidden`,
   *                         so exactly one mechanism truncates at any moment and the two
   *                         cannot disagree.
   *   :nth-child(n + 25)    plain `nth-child`, deliberately NOT the ladder's
   *                         `of :not([hidden])`. Nothing is hidden yet at this point, so
   *                         the two would count identically — and this one has to keep
   *                         working before the script exists.
   *
   * 25 IS `STEP + 1`, AND STEP LIVES IN scripts/card-index.ts. CSS cannot read it, so
   * the two are bound by this comment and its twin there. If one changes, both change.
   *
   * `:global()` on the child half for the reason every selector here needs it: the cards
   * are slotted and carry the caller's scope, not this component's.
   */
  html.js .card-masonry[data-truncate]:not([data-hydrated]) > :global(:nth-child(n + 25)) {
    display: none;
  }
</style>

<script>
  /**
   * Measure every VISIBLE card, hand it a row span, and place it in the column that is
   * shortest at that moment.
   *
   * ── GREEDY, AND PREFIX-BALANCED IS THE WHOLE REASON ──────────────────────────
   *
   * Walking the visible set in DOM order and always taking the shortest column leaves the
   * columns LEVEL AFTER EVERY PLACEMENT, not merely level at the end. Two consequences,
   * and both are things a threshold-based "fix the rag afterwards" pass would have to
   * chase and would get wrong:
   *
   *   - Revealing more cards keeps the balance. Truncation is always a DOM suffix, so a
   *     revealed set is a prefix plus more, and a prefix was already balanced.
   *   - A filter is just a different visible set, and this runs over whatever is left.
   *
   * TIES BREAK LEFTMOST — `<` rather than `<=`, which keeps the earliest index. That is
   * what guarantees the first n cards still read 1, 2, 3 across the top row, because every
   * column starts at zero.
   *
   * What it costs is that a card can sit further from its source position than the old
   * `nth-child` round-robin put it. DOM order is untouched; see the header on why that is
   * the property that actually matters.
   *
   * ── WHY THIS CANNOT HAPPEN AT BUILD TIME ─────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * A card's height depends on how its title and description wrap, which depends on the
   * column width and the loaded font. Only the browser knows. Prediction is not good
   * enough here: with `grid-auto-rows: 1px` a span is a hard track size, so any
   * under-estimate makes a card overflow into its neighbour.
   *
   * ── AND WHY IT MEASURES BEFORE IT APPLIES ────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Once the class is on, a card's grid area IS its span, so `offsetHeight` would return
   * the span rather than the content. Hence the remove / measure / add sequence.
   * `align-items: start` is what keeps the measured height the natural one rather than
   * the row's.
   */
  function enhance(grid: HTMLElement) {
    const cards = Array.from(grid.children) as HTMLElement[]

    function layout() {
      /* Back to the row grid so each card reports its own height. */
      grid.classList.remove('is-measured')

      /* Read from the cascade rather than hardcoded, so it follows `--rhythm-list` if
         that moves. Read while the row gap still applies — `is-measured` sets it to 0. */
      const gap = Math.round(parseFloat(getComputedStyle(grid).rowGap)) || 0

      /* Only what a filter left standing. A hidden card is `display: none`, so it holds
         no grid cell and reports no height — measuring one would write a span of just
         the gap. This is also the set the columns are assigned over, so a filtered view
         is balanced across its survivors rather than across the full corpus. */
      const visible = cards.filter((card) => !card.hidden)

      /*
       * `ceil` OF THE FRACTIONAL HEIGHT, not `offsetHeight`.
       *
       * `offsetHeight` is rounded to the nearest integer, so it rounds DOWN as often as
       * up — and a span one pixel short of its card is the one error this mechanism
       * cannot absorb, because with 1px rows the span is a hard track size and the card
       * overflows into its neighbour. Measured on Insights at the 2-column rung with
       * `offsetHeight`, the median gap came out at 30 rather than 32.
       *
       * `getBoundingClientRect().height` is fractional and `ceil` always errs long, so
       * gaps land in [32, 33) and overlap is impossible.
       *
       * Two passes on purpose: read every rect, THEN write. Interleaving would invalidate
       * layout on each write and force a reflow per card.
       */
      const rects = visible.map((card) => card.getBoundingClientRect())
      const spans = rects.map((rect) => Math.ceil(rect.height) + gap)

      /*
       * ── THE COLUMN COUNT COMES FROM GEOMETRY, NOT FROM `gridTemplateColumns` ────
       *
       * Reading it back from the computed style is the trap `d091932` hit: that property
       * reports IMPLICIT tracks too, so a stray placement makes it report a column that
       * should not exist, and the partition then latches onto it. Counting distinct left
       * edges asks the question the other way round — how many columns are cards ACTUALLY
       * sitting in — and the answer is the ladder's own, read back from the row-grid state
       * that is still in force here.
       *
       * `round` because subpixel track widths can jitter the left edge of cards in the
       * same column. Columns are a full gutter apart, so nothing can round together.
       *
       * FEWER CARDS THAN COLUMNS UNDER-REPORTS, and that is correct rather than tolerated:
       * two visible cards at the three-column rung give a count of 2, and greedy then puts
       * one in each — which is where they would have gone anyway. An empty set gives 0 and
       * the loop below does not run.
       */
      const columnCount = new Set(rects.map((rect) => Math.round(rect.left))).size

      /* One write pass. `heights` is the running total per column INCLUDING each card's
         trailing gap, since that is what a span is — so comparing them compares the real
         bottom edges. */
      const heights = new Array(columnCount).fill(0)
      visible.forEach((card, i) => {
        let shortest = 0
        for (let col = 1; col < columnCount; col += 1) {
          if (heights[col] < heights[shortest]) shortest = col
        }
        heights[shortest] += spans[i]

        card.style.setProperty('--card-span', String(spans[i]))
        /* 1-based: `grid-column` counts tracks from 1, and the ladder wrote 1/2/3. */
        card.style.setProperty('--card-column', String(shortest + 1))
      })

      grid.classList.add('is-measured')
    }

    /* Fonts first: the metric-matched fallback and the real face wrap differently, so
       measuring before the swap would bake in spans for the wrong face. */
    document.fonts.ready.then(layout)

    /*
     * Column width changes with the rung, and wrapping changes with width, so spans go
     * stale on resize. ResizeObserver on the grid rather than a window listener: it
     * catches a rung crossing and anything else that changes the grid's box.
     *
     * ── THE WIDTH GUARD IS NOT AN OPTIMISATION, IT BREAKS A FEEDBACK LOOP ──────
     *
     * ResizeObserver watches BOTH axes, and applying spans changes the grid's HEIGHT —
     * so an unguarded callback re-triggers itself. It would settle rather than spin,
     * since the second pass computes identical spans, but it costs a second full measure
     * of every card and is exactly the shape that logs "ResizeObserver loop completed
     * with undelivered notifications". Only a WIDTH change can alter wrapping.
     *
     * rAF rather than a timeout because this is layout work that should land before a
     * paint. It does not fire in a background tab, which is correct: the deferred
     * measure runs when the tab becomes visible, so a stale span is never displayed.
     */
    let queued = 0
    let lastWidth = 0
    new ResizeObserver((entries) => {
      const width = Math.round(entries[0].contentRect.width)
      if (width === lastWidth) return
      lastWidth = width
      cancelAnimationFrame(queued)
      queued = requestAnimationFrame(layout)
    }).observe(grid)

    /*
     * ── THE FILTER HOOK, AND IT IS PROVISIONAL ─────────────────────────────────
     *
     * On Insights `enhance()` RETURNS `layout` and the filter script calls it directly
     * after changing which cards are hidden. That cannot work across a component
     * boundary: an Astro component's <script> is its own module, so a page script holds
     * no reference to anything in here.
     *
     * A DOM event is the cheapest bridge that does not put a function on `window`, and
     * it is dispatched AT the grid, so a page with two of them addresses each one. The
     * extraction change should confirm this is the shape it wants before <FacetFilters>
     * is written against it — hence "provisional". Nothing dispatches it today.
     */
    grid.addEventListener('card-masonry:relayout', layout)
  }

  /* `querySelectorAll`, so a page may hold more than one grid. The data attribute rather
     than the class is the hook, so restyling never breaks the behaviour — and so this
     cannot collide with the `.masonry` that Insights still owns. */
  for (const grid of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-card-masonry]')) {
    enhance(grid)
  }
</script>
